      "text": "To convert the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to spherical coordinates, we need to use the following formulas:\n\n1. \\(\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\\)\n2. \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\) (adjusted for the correct quadrant)\n3. \\(\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\\)\n\nLet's calculate each of these step-by-step.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate \\(\\rho\\)\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(x = 0\\), \\(y = -3\\sqrt{3}\\), and \\(z = 3\\):\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{36}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho = 6\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate \\(\\theta\\)\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = -3\\sqrt{3}\\):\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n\\]\nSince \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y\\) is negative, the point lies in the fourth quadrant. The reference angle \\(\\theta_{\\text{ref}}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\theta_{\\text{ref}} = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}(\\sqrt{3}) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nHowever, in the fourth quadrant, the angle \\(\\theta\\) is:\n\\[\n\\theta = 2\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{6\\pi}{3} - \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate \\(\\phi\\)\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(z = 3\\) and \\(\\rho = 6\\):\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe spherical coordinates are \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\boxed{(6, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{3})}\\).",
