      "text": "To convert the rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z) = (0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\), we use the following formulas:\n\n1. \\(\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\\)\n2. \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\)\n3. \\(\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\\)\n\nLet's calculate each component step by step.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate \\(\\rho\\)**\n\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate \\(\\theta\\)**\n\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = -3\\sqrt{3}\\), the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) lies on the negative \\(y\\)-axis. Therefore, the angle \\(\\theta\\) is \\(\\pi\\) radians. We can verify this by noting that the point is directly below the origin on the \\(y\\)-axis, which corresponds to an angle of \\(\\pi\\) radians from the positive \\(z\\)-axis.\n\n**Step 3: Calculate \\(\\phi\\)**\n\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThe value of \\(\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(6, \\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3})}\n\\]",
