      "text": "To convert the point \\((0, -3 \\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\), we need to use the following formulas:\n\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the point \\((x, y, z) = (0, -3 \\sqrt{3}, 3)\\), we will calculate each of these values step by step.\n\n1. **Calculate \\(\\rho\\):**\n\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3 \\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\n2. **Calculate \\(\\theta\\):**\n\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3 \\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSince the point \\((0, -3 \\sqrt{3})\\) lies on the negative \\(y\\)-axis, \\(\\theta\\) must be in the fourth quadrant. The \\(\\tan^{-1}\\) function gives the angle in the fourth quadrant:\n\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3 \\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right) = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}\n\\]\n\n3. **Calculate \\(\\phi\\):**\n\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\phi = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\) are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3})}\n\\]",
